1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,552
Once a year, Timur runs a drill he calls the worst day.

2
00:00:03,602 --> 00:00:09,792
He hands a colleague a full copy of the card zone's database and says: get me one card number out of this.

3
00:00:09,842 --> 00:00:13,608
If the table held plain card numbers, the drill would take a second.

4
00:00:13,658 --> 00:00:17,664
If it held numbers locked with a key kept right next to them, it would take two.

5
00:00:17,714 --> 00:00:23,952
Most teams stop at that second version, and I think it is the most dangerous kind of safety, because it looks finished.

6
00:00:24,002 --> 00:00:26,184
The key must never sit next to the data.

7
00:00:26,234 --> 00:00:32,400
In Lesson 6, Vault came in to hold every key, so no key is ever typed into a file.

8
00:00:32,450 --> 00:00:36,312
This lesson gives Vault a second job: sealing card numbers.

9
00:00:36,362 --> 00:00:42,048
When Malika's buyer types a card, the card vault, the service that keeps cards, hands the number to Vault.

10
00:00:42,098 --> 00:00:43,968
Vault hands back a sealed value.

11
00:00:44,018 --> 00:00:46,560
The card vault keeps only that sealed value.

12
00:00:46,610 --> 00:00:48,432
The key never leaves Vault.

13
00:00:48,482 --> 00:00:50,544
Each seal is tied to its own card.

14
00:00:50,594 --> 00:00:54,168
A sealed value copied into another card's place will not open.

15
00:00:54,218 --> 00:00:57,576
So nobody can shuffle values around to trick the system.

16
00:00:57,626 --> 00:00:59,448
Then a puzzle.

17
00:00:59,498 --> 00:01:02,880
Sometimes the card zone must find a card again by its number.

18
00:01:02,930 --> 00:01:05,832
It must do that without opening every seal.

19
00:01:05,882 --> 00:01:09,264
So Vault also stamps each number with a fingerprint.

20
00:01:09,314 --> 00:01:12,456
The same number always gives the same fingerprint.

21
00:01:12,506 --> 00:01:15,336
The fingerprint cannot be turned back into the number.

22
00:01:15,386 --> 00:01:17,688
This stamp is called a blind index.

23
00:01:17,738 --> 00:01:20,736
The card zone looks up the fingerprint, never the number.

24
00:01:20,786 --> 00:01:23,016
The database guards its own door too.

25
00:01:23,066 --> 00:01:25,992
It refuses any card value that is not sealed.

26
00:01:26,042 --> 00:01:29,496
Even a mistake in the code cannot write a plain number into it.

27
00:01:29,546 --> 00:01:31,368
What if Vault is down?

28
00:01:31,418 --> 00:01:33,240
Then the card vault stops.

29
00:01:33,290 --> 00:01:37,272
It never guesses, and it never falls back to keeping a plain number.

30
00:01:37,322 --> 00:01:41,904
Stopping safely instead of carrying on unsafely is called fail closed.

31
00:01:41,954 --> 00:01:47,088
A number is opened for one reason only: to hand it to the card's network for a charge.

32
00:01:47,138 --> 00:01:52,128
Every use of card data is written down: what happened, who asked, and when.

33
00:01:52,178 --> 00:01:55,944
And a card nobody has used for the bank's chosen time is removed.

34
00:01:55,994 --> 00:01:59,664
Only a short note keeps its token and its masked number.

35
00:01:59,714 --> 00:02:02,136
Malika's buyer sees none of this.

36
00:02:02,186 --> 00:02:05,712
The buyer types a card into the card form and confirms the code.

37
00:02:05,762 --> 00:02:09,912
The form says "Card accepted" and empties its number field.

38
00:02:09,962 --> 00:02:16,200
The payment page then shows one line: the first six digits, a row of stars, and the last four.

39
00:02:16,250 --> 00:02:19,800
Beside them sit the card's network and the bank that issued it.

40
00:02:19,850 --> 00:02:24,048
That masked line is all anyone outside the card zone ever sees.

41
00:02:24,098 --> 00:02:27,600
During the drill, Timur stops Vault on purpose.

42
00:02:27,650 --> 00:02:31,680
A buyer types a card, and the form refuses it with a short message.

43
00:02:31,730 --> 00:02:34,776
Nothing is saved, sealed or unsealed.

44
00:02:34,826 --> 00:02:40,104
Timur's team gets an alert: a service the card zone depends on is unavailable.

45
00:02:40,154 --> 00:02:42,264
The alert names the part that failed.

46
00:02:42,314 --> 00:02:45,384
He starts Vault again, and the same card goes through.

47
00:02:45,434 --> 00:02:47,784
Then the colleague reports back on the copy.

48
00:02:47,834 --> 00:02:49,656
Sealed values.

49
00:02:49,706 --> 00:02:51,528
Fingerprints.

50
00:02:51,578 --> 00:02:53,400
Masked numbers.

51
00:02:53,450 --> 00:02:55,272
No keys.

52
00:02:55,322 --> 00:03:02,064
A backup is just as useless without the Vault that sealed it, so the bank keeps both, and keeps them apart.

53
00:03:02,114 --> 00:03:07,368
A stolen database, a stolen backup, or a curious insider all see noise.

54
00:03:07,418 --> 00:03:12,696
The colleague never gets a card number, and that is the only result the drill allows.

55
00:03:12,746 --> 00:03:15,360
Next: what a card's first digits tell you.
